  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059137/assets/cc0f19d50ccd27918d0ac005dec9a8cd27a4f104726ded0d07c71b571ad0522f.png)\n\nFind the area between the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-x}$, the $y$-axis and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1,$ as shown above.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{4-x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4-x \\\\ x & =4-{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\cdot y{|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4(1-0)-(\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =4-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
